.TH DAQ-READ-CONFIG 1 "2026-09-10" "mu2edaq-shifter-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
daq-read-config \- query the DAQ operations configuration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B daq-read-config
[\fB\-c\fR \fIconfig\fR]
[\fB\-z\fR \fIpartition\fR]
[\fB\-e\fR \fIenvironment\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
[\fIverb\fR]
.br
.B daq-read-config
\fB\-\-list\-verbs\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B daq-read-config
prints one value out of the DAQ operations YAML, selected by a
\fIcommand verb\fR.
.PP
This is the only thing in the toolkit that parses the operations
configuration. The shell commands do not read YAML themselves; they
call this tool once per value they need. The consequence for
maintenance is that
.B adding a new configuration field means adding a verb here,
not adding a parser to the calling script.
.PP
Text output is deliberately bare \(em no labels, no quoting \(em
because callers capture it with command substitution. A list is printed
space-separated on one line, which is what the callers' \fBfor\fR loops
expect. Use \fB\-\-json\fR for a structured consumer.
.SH OPTIONS
Configuration precedence is command line, then environment variable,
then .env file, then built-in default.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Operations YAML to read. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_CONFIG\fR. Default:
the first \fIdaq\-operations.yaml\fR on the config search path.
.TP
.BR \-z ", " \-\-partition " " \fINAME\fR
Partition to query. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_PARTITION\fR. Default:
\fIpartition_0\fR.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-environment " " \fINAME\fR
Environment to query. Environment: \fBMU2EDAQ_ENVIRONMENT\fR. For the
verbs that need one, this defaults to the partition's first active
environment.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit JSON instead of bare text.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-verbs
Print every verb and its description, tab-separated, and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the package version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary, including the verb table, and exit.
.SH COMMAND VERBS
.TP
.B print
The whole configuration. This is the default verb.
.TP
.B config-file
Path of the configuration file actually used, after the search.
.TP
.B config-path
The configuration search path, one directory per line. Works even when
no configuration file exists.
.TP
.B partitions
Names of every configured partition.
.TP
.B environments
Every environment defined in the partition.
.TP
.B active-envs
The partition's \fBactive_environments\fR, or every environment if the
partition does not list them.
.TP
.B base-release
The partition's base release path.
.TP
.B directory
The environment's \fBtest_rel_path\fR.
.TP
.B setup
The environment's \fBsetup_cmd\fR, arguments included.
.TP
.B port-offset
The environment's \fBport_offset\fR.
.TP
.B ots-port-offset
The partition's \fBots_port_offset\fR.
.TP
.B gateway-port
\fBots_port_offset\fR + \fBport_offset\fR, which is how ots derives the
environment's gateway port.
.TP
.B resource-manager-port
The ResourceManager port. Defaults to 1973, Mu2e being E-973.
.SH CONFIGURATION SEARCH PATH
Searched in order, first match wins:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$MU2EDAQ_CONFIG_DIR
\&./config
$MU2EDAQ_ROOT/config
~/.config/mu2edaq
/etc/mu2edaq
.fi
.RE
.PP
\fBMU2EDAQ_ROOT\fR is taken from the environment if set, otherwise
found by walking up from the installed package looking for
\fIconfig/daq\-operations.example.yaml\fR, otherwise
\fI~/daq\-shifter\-tools\fR.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The value was printed.
.TP
.B 1
The configuration file, partition, or environment could not be
resolved.
.TP
.B 2
The verb or an option was not recognised.
.SH EXAMPLES
Loop over a partition's active environments, as the shell commands do:
.PP
.RS
.nf
for env in $(daq-read-config -z partition_0 active-envs); do
    echo "$env -> $(daq-read-config -z partition_0 -e "$env" gateway-port)"
done
.fi
.RE
.PP
Find out which configuration file is in effect:
.PP
.RS
.nf
daq-read-config config-file
.fi
.RE
.PP
Dump everything for inspection:
.PP
.RS
.nf
daq-read-config --json print | jq .
.fi
.RE
